# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
slides = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Division Foundations', 'tagline': 'The Coconut Farm • Inverse Operations & Core Terminology', 'lecture': 'Welcome to the lush green coconut farm! Imagine harvesting fresh green coconuts and arranging them into neat rectangular arrays on the farm grass. If you plant six rows of coconut palms with eight palms in each row, multiplication tells us that six times eight equals forty-eight coconut palms in total. Division is the direct mathematical inverse or reverse operation of multiplication. If a farmer collects forty-eight coconuts and packs them equally into six burlap sacks, how many coconuts go into each sack? We divide forty-eight by six, which gives exactly eight coconuts per sack! In every division problem, three essential terms establish the relationship. The total quantity being divided is called the Dividend, symbolized by the letter N. The number of equal groups we are dividing into is called the Divisor, symbolized by the letter D. The resulting answer, showing the size of each individual share, is called the Quotient, symbolized by the letter Q. When there are no leftovers, the fundamental relationship is expressed as: Dividend equals Divisor multiplied by Quotient. That is, forty-eight equals six multiplied by eight. If you know any multiplication fact, you instantly hold the master key to two related division facts, establishing numerical harmony across arithmetic.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥥</span> The Coconut Farm Array Model\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Multiplication builds arrays by grouping rows and columns; division partitions the total back into equal rows:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center text-slate-300 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                        <span>Multiplication Fact:</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">6 rows × 8 palms = 48 Palms</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center text-slate-300 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                        <span>Division Fact 1:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">48 ÷ 6 = 8 palms per row</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Division Fact 2:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">48 ÷ 8 = 6 rows of palms</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>📐</span> Core Mathematical Terminology\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-500/10 via-cyan-500/10 to-emerald-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-center my-2">\n                        <div class="text-lg font-black text-amber-300 font-mono">Dividend (N) = Divisor (D) × Quotient (Q)</div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">48 (N) = 6 (D) × 8 (Q)</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-1.5 leading-relaxed">\n                        <li><strong>• Dividend (N):</strong> The grand total amount being shared or partitioned.</li>\n                        <li><strong>• Divisor (D):</strong> The number of groups being created or the size of each part.</li>\n                        <li><strong>• Quotient (Q):</strong> The resulting number of items allocated to each equal share.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Inverse Principle:</strong> Division undoes multiplication. If A × B = C, then C ÷ A = B and C ÷ B = A!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c1_1', 'question': 'A coconut farmer harvests 72 coconuts and packs them equally into 9 wooden crates. In the equation 72 ÷ 9 = 8, what is the mathematical term for 72?', 'options': ['Quotient', 'Divisor', 'Dividend', 'Remainder'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Identify the total number being divided.', 'explanation': 'In 72 ÷ 9 = 8, the total quantity being divided (72) is called the Dividend. The divisor is 9, and the quotient is 8.'}, {'id': 'c1_2', 'question': 'If 7 × 12 = 84, which of the following is a valid related division statement?', 'options': ['84 ÷ 12 = 7', '84 ÷ 7 = 14', '12 ÷ 7 = 84', '84 × 7 = 12'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide the product (84) by one of the factors to get the other factor.', 'explanation': 'From 7 × 12 = 84, we can derive two division facts: 84 ÷ 7 = 12, and 84 ÷ 12 = 7.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Fact Triangles', 'tagline': 'Let Us Play • Number Fact Triangles & Related Division Statements', 'lecture': 'Mathematical operations are deeply interconnected through family relationships. To visualize and master table fluency, we use an engaging tool called the Number Fact Triangle. Picture a triangle with three circular nodes at its vertices and a square value box at its peak. The two bottom corner nodes contain two factors, while the top peak box contains their product. For example, if the bottom corners contain thirty and thirty, multiplying thirty by thirty yields nine hundred at the top. Because multiplication and division are inverse operations, this single triangle immediately produces two complementary division facts: nine hundred divided by thirty equals thirty, and nine hundred divided by the other thirty also equals thirty. Now consider another triangle with twenty and forty at the bottom. Their product at the peak is eight hundred. From this, we read two division pathways: eight hundred divided by twenty equals forty, and eight hundred divided by forty equals twenty. Fact triangles turn division into a logical game of finding the missing family member. If the peak is six hundred and thirty and one bottom corner is seventy, dividing six hundred and thirty by seventy reveals nine in the missing corner. Connecting operations strengthens mental flexibility.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🔺</span> The Fact Triangle Family Structure\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    A single fact triangle generates one multiplication fact and two inverse division facts:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 space-y-2 text-xs font-mono">\n                    <div class="text-center p-2 rounded bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800">\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block text-sm">PEAK = 900</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400 text-[10px]">Product Box</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between px-4 text-cyan-300 font-bold">\n                        <span>Node A: 30</span>\n                        <span>Node B: 30</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="border-t border-slate-700 pt-2 space-y-1 text-slate-300 text-[11px]">\n                        <div>• 30 × 30 = 900 (Multiplication)</div>\n                        <div>• 900 ÷ 30 = 30 (Division Fact 1)</div>\n                        <div>• 900 ÷ 30 = 30 (Division Fact 2)</div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🧩</span> Missing Node Fact Triangle Logic\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                        When one node is missing, choose the correct inverse operation to solve:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                            <div class="font-bold text-emerald-400">Peak = 630, Bottom Left = 70:</div>\n                            <div class="text-slate-300 mt-1 font-mono">Missing Node = 630 ÷ 70 = <strong class="text-cyan-300">9</strong></div>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                            <div class="font-bold text-amber-400">Peak = 480, Bottom Right = 6:</div>\n                            <div class="text-slate-300 mt-1 font-mono">Missing Node = 480 ÷ 6 = <strong class="text-cyan-300">80</strong></div>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200 mt-3">\n                    🔍 <strong>Rule of Thumb:</strong> To find the peak, multiply the bottom corners. To find a bottom corner, divide the peak by the known corner!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c2_1', 'question': 'In a fact triangle, the peak product box displays 720 and the bottom-left node contains 80. What number belongs in the bottom-right node?', 'options': ['9', '90', '8', '640'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide the top number by the bottom-left node: 720 ÷ 80.', 'explanation': '720 ÷ 80 = (72 ÷ 8) = 9. So the missing factor is 9.'}, {'id': 'c2_2', 'question': 'Which two division statements can be derived from the multiplication fact 25 × 40 = 1,000?', 'options': ['1,000 ÷ 25 = 40 and 1,000 ÷ 40 = 25', '1,000 ÷ 20 = 50 and 1,000 ÷ 50 = 20', '1,000 ÷ 4 = 250 and 1,000 ÷ 250 = 4', '1,000 ÷ 10 = 100 and 1,000 ÷ 100 = 10'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'The product 1,000 divided by either factor gives the other factor.', 'explanation': 'From 25 × 40 = 1,000, dividing 1,000 by 25 yields 40, and dividing 1,000 by 40 yields 25.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Trailing Zeros', 'tagline': 'Patterns in Division & Place Value • Powers of 10 & Zero Cancellation', 'lecture': 'Dividing numbers that end in trailing zeros reveals one of the most powerful shortcuts in arithmetic. The decimal number system is organized around base ten, where moving one column to the left multiplies a digit by ten, and moving one column to the right divides it by ten. When dividing a number by ten, every digit shifts one place value to the right, which cancels out one trailing zero. For example, six hundred divided by ten equals sixty. Dividing by one hundred shifts every digit two places to the right, canceling two trailing zeros—so six hundred divided by one hundred equals six. When both the dividend and the divisor end in trailing zeros, we can cancel an equal number of zeros from both sides before dividing. Consider seven thousand two hundred divided by ninety. Both numbers share at least one trailing zero. We cancel one zero from ninety and one zero from seven thousand two hundred, turning the problem into seven hundred and twenty divided by nine. We know seventy-two divided by nine is eight, so seven hundred and twenty divided by nine is eighty! Remember the balance rule: you must cancel the exact same number of trailing zeros from both the dividend and divisor.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>⚡</span> The Zero Cancellation Shortcut\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Cancel an equal number of trailing zeros from both the dividend and the divisor:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs font-mono">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>7,200 ÷ 90</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">= 80</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">Cancel 1 zero: 720 ÷ 9 = 80</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>4,500 ÷ 500</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">= 9</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">Cancel 2 zeros: 45 ÷ 5 = 9</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between font-bold text-slate-200">\n                            <span>28,000 ÷ 70</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">= 400</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">Cancel 1 zero: 2,800 ÷ 7 = 400</div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>📊</span> Place-Value Shift Grid\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Dividing by powers of 10 shifts digits to the right across standard place values:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 text-xs font-mono space-y-1">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                            <span>OPERATION</span>\n                            <span>SHIFT</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400">RESULT</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>5,000 ÷ 10</span>\n                            <span>1 Place Right</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-cyan-300">500</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>5,000 ÷ 100</span>\n                            <span>2 Places Right</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-cyan-300">50</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>5,000 ÷ 1,000</span>\n                            <span>3 Places Right</span>\n                            <span class="font-bold text-cyan-300">5</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-rose-950/40 border border-rose-800/40 text-[11px] text-rose-200 mt-3">\n                    ⚠️ <strong>Common Trap:</strong> Never cancel more zeros than the divisor possesses! In 3,000 ÷ 50, cancel only ONE zero: 300 ÷ 5 = 60 (not 6).\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c3_1', 'question': 'What is the value of 5,600 ÷ 70?', 'options': ['8', '80', '800', '8,000'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Cancel one zero from both numbers: 560 ÷ 7.', 'explanation': '5,600 ÷ 70 = (5,600 ÷ 10) ÷ (70 ÷ 10) = 560 ÷ 7 = 80.'}, {'id': 'c3_2', 'question': 'A shopkeeper has ₹4,200 and wants to convert the entire amount into ₹100 currency notes. How many ₹100 notes will he receive?', 'options': ['420 notes', '42 notes', '402 notes', '4,200 notes'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Divide ₹4,200 by 100 by canceling two trailing zeros.', 'explanation': '4,200 ÷ 100 = 42 notes. Dividing by 100 cancels two trailing zeros.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Place Value Division', 'tagline': 'Real-World Problem Solving • Rate Calculations & Bonus Distribution', 'lecture': 'Division with multiples of ten and hundred plays a vital role in everyday planning, sports journeys, and commercial farm accounts. Consider a long-distance cyclist embarking on an adventurous coastal journey across the Western Ghats. The cyclist covers a total distance of one hundred and sixty kilometres in twenty days, pedaling the exact same distance every single day. How many kilometres does the cyclist travel daily? We divide one hundred and sixty by twenty. By canceling one trailing zero from both numbers, one hundred and sixty divided by twenty simplifies to sixteen divided by two, which equals eight kilometres per day! Now imagine a prosperous coconut farming cooperative that earns a seasonal surplus and decides to distribute an equal cash bonus among its thirty dedicated farm workers. If the total bonus fund is twenty-one thousand rupees, how much money does each worker take home? We divide twenty-one thousand by thirty. Canceling one zero from each side transforms the expression into twenty-one hundred divided by three. Since twenty-one divided by three is seven, twenty-one hundred divided by three yields seven hundred rupees per worker. Place value shifts allow us to manage community resources and solve real-world logistical challenges with speed and precision.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🚴</span> Coastal Cycling Tour Rate\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Calculate daily distance: Total Distance ÷ Total Days:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Total Journey:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">160 km</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Duration:</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">20 days</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Daily Rate:</span>\n                        <span>160 ÷ 20 = 8 km/day</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>💰</span> Equal Bonus Allocation\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Sharing a ₹21,000 profit bonus among 30 farm workers:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Bonus Fund:</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">₹21,000</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Workers:</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">30 workers</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                            <span>Per Worker:</span>\n                            <span>21,000 ÷ 30 = ₹700</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-[11px] text-emerald-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Mental Step:</strong> Cancel 1 zero: 2,100 ÷ 3. Since 21 ÷ 3 = 7, append the two remaining zeros to get 700!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c4_1', 'question': 'A wholesale grain dealer has 3,500 kg of wheat to pack into bags holding 50 kg each. How many bags does the dealer need?', 'options': ['70 bags', '700 bags', '50 bags', '35 bags'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Cancel one zero from 3,500 and 50: 350 ÷ 5.', 'explanation': '3,500 ÷ 50 = 350 ÷ 5 = 70 bags.'}, {'id': 'c4_2', 'question': 'A school library receives 1,800 new books and distributes them equally among 60 classrooms. How many books does each classroom receive?', 'options': ['300 books', '30 books', '18 books', '60 books'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Cancel one zero from 1,800 and 60: 180 ÷ 6.', 'explanation': '1,800 ÷ 60 = 180 ÷ 6 = 30 books per classroom.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Convenient Splitting', 'tagline': 'Mental Strategies for Division • Additive & Subtractive Dividend Partitioning', 'lecture': 'When faced with multi-digit division problems, mental mathematicians rarely perform vertical long division on paper. Instead, they use convenient splitting, which applies the distributive property of division over addition and subtraction. To divide a complex dividend, we decompose it into friendly numbers that are readily recognized multiples of the divisor. Let us solve one thousand two hundred and forty-eight divided by six. Twelve hundred and forty-eight easily splits into twelve hundred plus forty-eight. Both parts are exact multiples of six! Twelve hundred divided by six equals two hundred, and forty-eight divided by six equals eight. Adding two hundred and eight yields two hundred and eight! Splitting also works brilliantly using subtraction when a number is just below a friendly round benchmark. Consider one thousand nine hundred and ninety-two divided by four. Notice that one thousand nine hundred and ninety-two is only eight less than two thousand! We express it as two thousand minus eight. Now divide each part by four: two thousand divided by four equals five hundred, and eight divided by four equals two. Subtracting two from five hundred gives four hundred and ninety-eight! Convenient splitting turns intimidating calculations into fast, elegant mental solutions.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>➕</span> Additive Splitting: 1,248 ÷ 6\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Split 1,248 into two friendly multiples of 6: 1,200 and 48:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="text-cyan-300">1,248 ÷ 6 = (1,200 ÷ 6) + (48 ÷ 6)</div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-t border-slate-700 pt-1">\n                        <span>Part 1: 1,200 ÷ 6</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">= 200</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Part 2: 48 ÷ 6</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">= 8</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Combine: 200 + 8</span>\n                        <span>= 208</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>➖</span> Subtractive Splitting: 1,992 ÷ 4\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        When close to a round thousand, round up and subtract the excess:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                        <div class="text-cyan-300">1,992 ÷ 4 = (2,000 ÷ 4) - (8 ÷ 4)</div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-t border-slate-700 pt-1">\n                            <span>Part 1: 2,000 ÷ 4</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">= 500</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Part 2: 8 ÷ 4</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">= 2</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                            <span>Subtract: 500 - 2</span>\n                            <span>= 498</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Selection Key:</strong> Choose multiples ending in zeros (like 1,200 or 2,000) so mental division takes only a split second!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c5_1', 'question': 'Using additive convenient splitting, what is the fastest way to calculate 1,535 ÷ 5 mentally?', 'options': ['(1,500 ÷ 5) + (35 ÷ 5) = 300 + 7 = 307', '(1,000 ÷ 5) + (535 ÷ 5) = 200 + 107 = 307', '(1,500 ÷ 5) + (35 ÷ 5) = 30 + 7 = 37', '(2,000 ÷ 5) - (465 ÷ 5) = 400 - 93 = 307'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Split 1,535 into 1,500 and 35.', 'explanation': '1,535 splits into 1,500 + 35. 1,500 ÷ 5 = 300, and 35 ÷ 5 = 7. Sum = 300 + 7 = 307.'}, {'id': 'c5_2', 'question': 'Use subtractive splitting to find 2,991 ÷ 3:', 'options': ['(3,000 ÷ 3) - (9 ÷ 3) = 1,000 - 3 = 997', '(3,000 ÷ 3) - (1 ÷ 3) = 1,000 - 1 = 999', '(2,700 ÷ 3) + (291 ÷ 3) = 900 + 97 = 997', '(3,000 ÷ 3) - (9 ÷ 3) = 100 - 3 = 97'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '2,991 is 9 less than 3,000. Write as (3,000 - 9) ÷ 3.', 'explanation': '2,991 = 3,000 - 9. (3,000 ÷ 3) - (9 ÷ 3) = 1,000 - 3 = 997.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Repeated Halving', 'tagline': 'Mental Strategies for Division • Powers of 2 (÷4 and ÷8) Shortcuts', 'lecture': 'Dividing by numbers that are powers of two—specifically four and eight—can be accomplished rapidly without standard long division by using repeated halving. The number four is two times two, which means that dividing any quantity by four is mathematically identical to finding half of the number, and then finding half of that result again! Let us test this with five hundred and twenty-eight divided by four. Step one: find half of five hundred and twenty-eight. Half of five hundred is two hundred and fifty, and half of twenty-eight is fourteen, giving two hundred and sixty-four. Step two: find half of two hundred and sixty-four. Half of two hundred is one hundred, and half of sixty-four is thirty-two, yielding one hundred and thirty-two! Therefore, five hundred and twenty-eight divided by four equals one hundred and thirty-two. What about dividing by eight? Since eight equals two times two times two, dividing by eight simply requires halving three times in succession! For instance, to divide nine hundred and sixty by eight: first half gives four hundred and eighty, second half gives two hundred and forty, and third half gives one hundred and twenty. Repeated halving bypasses bulky calculations with lightning-fast mental steps.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>✂️</span> Dividing by 4: Two Halves\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Dividing by 4 = Halving twice: (N ÷ 2) ÷ 2:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">Calculate: 528 ÷ 4</div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-t border-slate-700 pt-1">\n                        <span>First Half (÷ 2):</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">528 ÷ 2 = 264</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-t border-slate-700 pt-1">\n                        <span>Second Half (÷ 2):</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">264 ÷ 2 = 132</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-1">Result: 528 ÷ 4 = 132</div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>✂️✂️</span> Dividing by 8: Three Halves\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Dividing by 8 = Halving three times: ((N ÷ 2) ÷ 2) ÷ 2:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                        <div class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">Calculate: 960 ÷ 8</div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>1st Half:</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300">960 ÷ 2 = 480</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>2nd Half:</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300">480 ÷ 2 = 240</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>3rd Half:</span>\n                            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">240 ÷ 2 = 120</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-[11px] text-emerald-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Summary Axiom:</strong> ÷ 4 = Half of Half. ÷ 8 = Half of Half of Half. Halving even numbers is naturally intuitive!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c6_1', 'question': 'Use repeated halving to calculate 744 ÷ 4:', 'options': ['186', '184', '196', '372'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Half of 744 is 372. Half of 372 is 186.', 'explanation': 'First half: 744 ÷ 2 = 372. Second half: 372 ÷ 2 = 186. So 744 ÷ 4 = 186.'}, {'id': 'c6_2', 'question': 'What is 1,280 ÷ 8 using three successive halves?', 'options': ['320', '160', '80', '640'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Half 1 = 640. Half 2 = 320. Half 3 = 160.', 'explanation': '1,280 ÷ 2 = 640. 640 ÷ 2 = 320. 320 ÷ 2 = 160. So 1,280 ÷ 8 = 160.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Partial Quotients', 'tagline': "Susie's Farm in Kerala • Repeated Subtraction vs Optimized Partial Quotients", 'lecture': "On Susie's coconut farm in Kerala, workers collect five hundred and eighty-two coconuts that must be bundled into sets of six for transportation to the wholesale market. How can we divide five hundred and eighty-two by six? Susie and her cousin Sunitha use two different partial quotient strategies. Susie uses small, cautious chunks: she subtracts fifty bundles of six, which is three hundred coconuts, leaving two hundred and eighty-two. Then she subtracts twenty bundles of six, which is one hundred and twenty, leaving one hundred and sixty-two. She continues chipping away until all coconuts are bundled. Sunitha, however, is an optimization master! Sunitha looks at five hundred and eighty-two and asks: what is the largest friendly multiple of six close to five hundred and eighty-two? She knows ninety times six is five hundred and forty! So Sunitha takes a giant leap of ninety bundles at once. Five hundred and eighty-two minus five hundred and forty leaves only forty-two coconuts! From table facts, seven times six is forty-two. Adding her two partial quotients—ninety plus seven—Sunitha finishes the division in just two clean steps, reaching ninety-seven bundles! Taking larger partial quotient leaps saves time and prevents computational errors.", 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🌴</span> Sunitha\'s Optimized Partial Quotients (582 ÷ 6)\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Take large, confident multiples of 6 to reach the answer in two steps:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Total Coconuts:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">582</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-amber-300">\n                        <span>Leap 1 (90 bundles × 6):</span>\n                        <span>- 540 (Partial Q: 90)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Remainder to bundle:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">42</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-amber-300">\n                        <span>Leap 2 (7 bundles × 6):</span>\n                        <span>- 42 (Partial Q: 7)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Total Quotient (90 + 7):</span>\n                        <span>= 97 Bundles</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>⚖️</span> Susie\'s vs Sunitha\'s Methods\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Both methods are mathematically sound, but larger partial quotients are much more efficient:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                            <span class="font-bold text-slate-300">Susie\'s Cautious Chunks:</span>\n                            <div class="text-slate-400 text-[11px] mt-0.5">Subtracts 50 × 6, then 20 × 6, then 20 × 6, then 7 × 6 (4 steps).</div>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-400">Sunitha\'s Optimized Leaps:</span>\n                            <div class="text-slate-300 text-[11px] mt-0.5">Subtracts 90 × 6 = 540, then 7 × 6 = 42 (2 fast steps!).</div>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Strategy:</strong> Look for the largest tens multiple first (10×, 20×, 50×, 100×) to make the biggest dent in the dividend.\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c7_1', 'question': 'To solve 438 ÷ 6 using partial quotients, what is the largest convenient tens multiple of 6 to subtract first?', 'options': ['70 × 6 = 420', '60 × 6 = 360', '50 × 6 = 300', '80 × 6 = 480'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Find a multiple of 6 close to 43 without exceeding it: 6 × 7 = 42, so 6 × 70 = 420.', 'explanation': '6 × 70 = 420, which is close to 438 without exceeding it. 80 × 6 = 480 is too large. So 70 is the optimal first leap.'}, {'id': 'c7_2', 'question': 'After subtracting 70 × 6 = 420 from 438, 18 remains. What is the final quotient of 438 ÷ 6?', 'options': ['73', '72', '74', '68'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '18 ÷ 6 = 3. Add 70 + 3.', 'explanation': '18 ÷ 6 = 3. Total quotient = 70 + 3 = 73.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Division Algorithm', 'tagline': 'Let Us Learn to Divide • Remainders & The Fundamental Division Equation', 'lecture': 'In real life, harvested items cannot always be divided into perfectly equal shares without something left over. When forty-seven tender coconuts are distributed equally among five eager school children, each child receives nine coconuts, but two coconuts remain in the basket because two is smaller than five and cannot be shared equally. This leftover quantity is called the Remainder, symbolized by the letter R. This observation leads directly to the celebrated Fundamental Division Algorithm of arithmetic: Dividend equals Divisor multiplied by Quotient plus Remainder. In mathematical symbols: N equals D times Q plus R. In our coconut example: forty-seven equals five times nine plus two, because five times nine is forty-five, and forty-five plus two is forty-seven. Notice the unbreakable mathematical condition governing all remainders: the remainder must always be strictly less than the divisor, and greater than or equal to zero! In symbols, zero is less than or equal to R, which is strictly less than D. If your remainder is ever equal to or greater than the divisor, it means you can form at least one more full group, proving your quotient was too small. The division algorithm provides an airtight algebraic equation to verify whether your long division calculation is completely correct.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥥</span> Sharing 47 Coconuts Among 5 Children\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Each child receives an equal share, leaving a leftover remainder:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                        <span>Dividend (N):</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">47 Coconuts</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                        <span>Divisor (D):</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">5 Children</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1">\n                        <span>Quotient (Q):</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">9 Coconuts each</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Remainder (R):</span>\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">2 Coconuts left over</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>⚖️</span> The Fundamental Division Equation\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-gradient-to-r from-cyan-500/10 via-emerald-500/10 to-amber-500/10 border border-cyan-500/30 text-center my-2 font-mono">\n                        <div class="text-base font-black text-emerald-300">N = (D × Q) + R</div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-amber-300 mt-1">47 = (5 × 9) + 2 = 45 + 2</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-1">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-amber-300">The Golden Remainder Condition:</div>\n                        <div class="font-mono text-cyan-300">0 ≤ R < D</div>\n                        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400">The remainder must be strictly smaller than the divisor!</div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    🔍 <strong>Verification Rule:</strong> Always check: Divisor × Quotient + Remainder MUST equal Dividend. If it does not match, re-check your subtraction!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c8_1', 'question': 'When dividing a whole number by 7, which of the following could NEVER be the remainder?', 'options': ['0', '3', '6', '7'], 'answer': 3, 'hint': 'The remainder must always be strictly less than the divisor (R < 7).', 'explanation': 'Because 0 ≤ R < D, dividing by 7 can only produce remainders of 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6. A remainder of 7 means you can make another group of 7!'}, {'id': 'c8_2', 'question': 'A student performs division and finds Divisor = 8, Quotient = 15, Remainder = 5. What was the Dividend?', 'options': ['120', '125', '115', '128'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Use the division equation: Dividend = (Divisor × Quotient) + Remainder.', 'explanation': 'Dividend = (8 × 15) + 5 = 120 + 5 = 125.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Group Allocation', 'tagline': 'Application Word Problems • Trip Buses, Catering Logistics & Note Combos', 'lecture': 'Division with remainders is not merely an abstract classroom exercise; it is an indispensable tool for managing real-world community events, transport systems, and trip logistics. Suppose a school plans an excursion to a botanical garden for two hundred and forty-eight students and teachers. If each chartered bus has a seating capacity of fifty passengers, how many buses must the principal book? Dividing two hundred and forty-eight by fifty gives a quotient of four buses with a remainder of forty-eight passengers! If the school booked only four buses, forty-eight students would be left behind crying at the school gate! In logistics, when a remainder occurs, we must round up to the next whole bus. The school must book five buses, even though the fifth bus will have two empty seats. Another practical scenario involves currency note combinations. Suppose you need to pay five hundred and twenty rupees using only fifty-rupee and twenty-rupee notes. Dividing five hundred and twenty by fifty gives ten fifty-rupee notes with a remainder of twenty rupees. That remaining twenty rupees is paid with exactly one twenty-rupee note! In group allocation, interpreting the remainder correctly makes the critical difference between seamless organization and logistical chaos.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🚌</span> School Excursion Bus Logistics\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    248 passengers divided into 50-seater buses requires remainder rounding up:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Total Passengers:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">248</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Bus Capacity:</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">50 seats</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Mathematical Division:</span>\n                        <span>248 ÷ 50 = 4 R 48</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Buses to Book:</span>\n                        <span>5 Buses (Ceiling Rounding)</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>💵</span> Note Combination: Pay ₹520\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Decompose using ₹50 and ₹20 notes systematically:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Primary Notes (₹50):</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">10 notes = ₹500</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Remainder:</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">₹520 - ₹500 = ₹20</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-bold">\n                            <span>Secondary Notes (₹20):</span>\n                            <span>1 note = ₹20</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Logistics Golden Rule:</strong> When transporting people or fragile goods, any non-zero remainder requires adding one extra container or vehicle!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c9_1', 'question': 'A caterer has 175 samosas for a party. If each serving tray holds 24 samosas, how many trays are required to serve all 175 samosas?', 'options': ['7 trays', '8 trays', '9 trays', '6 trays'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '175 ÷ 24 = 7 with a remainder of 7 samosas. Can we leave 7 samosas off the table?', 'explanation': '175 ÷ 24 = 7 with remainder 7. 7 trays hold 168 samosas. To accommodate the remaining 7 samosas, an 8th tray is required.'}, {'id': 'c9_2', 'question': 'Five friends share the cost of a restaurant dinner totaling ₹1,284 equally. If the bill cannot be split into paise, how much does each pay, and what is the small excess remainder?', 'options': ['₹256 each with ₹4 remainder', '₹250 each with ₹34 remainder', '₹255 each with ₹9 remainder', '₹257 each with ₹0 remainder'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide 1,284 by 5.', 'explanation': '1,284 ÷ 5 = 256 with a remainder of 4. Each friend pays ₹256, leaving ₹4 to be covered.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Coconut Oil Math', 'tagline': 'Kalpavruksha Coconut Oil • Bulk Oil Extraction Yields & Revenue', 'lecture': 'On the Kalpavruksha farm in south India, dry mature coconuts called copra are processed in an oil mill to produce pure coconut oil. The farm records show that exactly eight coconuts yield one litre of rich, fragrant oil. During the peak harvest season, the farm workers bring in four thousand three hundred and seventy-six coconuts for oil processing. How many litres of coconut oil will be extracted? We must divide four thousand three hundred and seventy-six by eight. Let us use place value division: forty-three divided by eight gives five with a remainder of three. Bring down seven to make thirty-seven; thirty-seven divided by eight gives four with a remainder of five. Bring down six to make fifty-six; fifty-six divided by eight gives seven with zero remainder! The mill extracts exactly five hundred and forty-seven litres of coconut oil. Next, the farm sells this oil in sealed tin containers at two hundred and forty rupees per litre. To calculate the total farm revenue, we multiply five hundred and forty-seven litres by two hundred and forty rupees, yielding one hundred and thirty-one thousand two hundred and eighty rupees! Dividing large numbers allows farmers to monitor processing efficiency and project seasonal income.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥥</span> Copra Oil Extraction (4,376 ÷ 8)\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    8 coconuts = 1 litre of cold-pressed oil:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Total Coconuts:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">4,376</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Rate of Extraction:</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">8 coconuts / Litre</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Division:</span>\n                        <span>4,376 ÷ 8</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Total Oil Yield:</span>\n                        <span>547 Litres</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>💰</span> Commercial Farm Revenue Ledger\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Selling 547 litres of oil at ₹240 per litre:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Volume Sold:</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">547 Litres</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Selling Price:</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">₹240 / Litre</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                            <span>Total Farm Revenue:</span>\n                            <span>₹1,31,280</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-[11px] text-emerald-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Step Check:</strong> 547 × 8 = 4,376 coconuts. 547 × ₹240 = ₹1,31,280. The division and multiplication verify each other perfectly!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c10_1', 'question': 'A smaller organic farm uses 6 coconuts to produce 1 litre of virgin coconut oil. How many litres of oil will they extract from 2,832 coconuts?', 'options': ['472 Litres', '462 Litres', '482 Litres', '452 Litres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide 2,832 by 6: 28 ÷ 6 = 4 R 4; 43 ÷ 6 = 7 R 1; 12 ÷ 6 = 2.', 'explanation': '2,832 ÷ 6 = 472 litres of oil.'}, {'id': 'c10_2', 'question': 'If 472 litres of virgin oil is sold at ₹300 per litre, what is the gross earnings of the organic farm?', 'options': ['₹1,41,600', '₹1,40,000', '₹1,42,600', '₹1,38,600'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Multiply 472 by 300: 472 × 3 = 1,416. Append two zeros.', 'explanation': '472 × ₹300 = ₹1,41,600.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Two-Digit Divisors', 'tagline': 'Kalpavruksha Farm Sales • Dividing by 23 and 35 via Estimation', 'lecture': 'When dealing with wholesale agricultural sales and cargo transport, the divisor is frequently a two-digit number. Dividing by a two-digit divisor, such as twenty-three or thirty-five, requires rounding to estimate trial quotient digits with confidence. Consider the Kalpavruksha coir processing unit, where shredded coconut husk is pressed into uniform export bales weighing twenty-three kilograms each. If the farm workers accumulate nine thousand nine hundred and thirteen kilograms of dry husk, how many complete bales can they press? We must divide nine thousand nine hundred and thirteen by twenty-three. Let us estimate: twenty-three is close to twenty. To divide ninety-nine by twenty-three, think of ninety divided by twenty, which suggests four. Multiplying twenty-three by four gives ninety-two. Ninety-nine minus ninety-two leaves seven. Bring down one to make seventy-one. Seventy-one divided by twenty-three suggests three; twenty-three times three is sixty-nine. Seventy-one minus sixty-nine leaves two. Bring down three to make twenty-three; twenty-three divided by twenty-three is exactly one! The workers press exactly four hundred and thirty-one bales with zero husk left over. Similarly, dividing eight thousand eight hundred and ninety tender coconuts into crates of thirty-five yields two hundred and fifty-four full crates. Rounding the two-digit divisor to the nearest friendly ten helps you choose trial quotient digits accurately without tedious trial-and-error guessing.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🌾</span> Coconut Husk Bales (9,913 ÷ 23)\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Round divisor 23 to 20 to estimate trial quotient digits:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Total Husk:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">9,913 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Bale Weight:</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">23 kg / bale</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-slate-300 space-y-0.5 text-[11px]">\n                        <div>• 99 ÷ 23 ➔ 4 (4 × 23 = 92; Rem = 7)</div>\n                        <div>• 71 ÷ 23 ➔ 3 (3 × 23 = 69; Rem = 2)</div>\n                        <div>• 23 ÷ 23 ➔ 1 (1 × 23 = 23; Rem = 0)</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Total Bales Pressed:</span>\n                        <span>431 Bales</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🥥</span> Tender Coconut Crates (8,890 ÷ 35)\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Packing 8,890 tender coconuts into crates of 35 each:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>88 ÷ 35:</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300">2 (2 × 35 = 70; Rem = 18)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>189 ÷ 35:</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300">5 (5 × 35 = 175; Rem = 14)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>140 ÷ 35:</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300">4 (4 × 35 = 140; Rem = 0)</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                            <span>Total Crates:</span>\n                            <span>254 Crates</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Trial Digit Rule:</strong> If trial digit × divisor exceeds the working dividend, reduce the trial digit by 1 and re-multiply!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c11_1', 'question': 'When dividing 7,560 by 24, what is the best estimated trial digit for the first step (75 ÷ 24)?', 'options': ['3 (since 24 × 3 = 72)', '4 (since 24 × 4 = 96)', '2 (since 24 × 2 = 48)', '5 (since 24 × 5 = 120)'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think of 75 ÷ 25 ≈ 3. Check 24 × 3 = 72.', 'explanation': '24 × 3 = 72, which fits inside 75 with remainder 3. 24 × 4 = 96 is too large. So 3 is the correct first quotient digit.'}, {'id': 'c11_2', 'question': 'What is the complete quotient of 7,560 ÷ 24?', 'options': ['315', '325', '310', '305'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '75 ÷ 24 = 3 R 3; 36 ÷ 24 = 1 R 12; 120 ÷ 24 = 5.', 'explanation': '75 ÷ 24 = 3 (rem 3); 36 ÷ 24 = 1 (rem 12); 120 ÷ 24 = 5 (rem 0). Total = 315.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Standard Algorithm', 'tagline': 'Division Using Place Value • The 4-Step Cycle: Divide, Multiply, Subtract, Bring Down', 'lecture': 'The traditional vertical long division method is an algorithm celebrated worldwide for its compact efficiency. It breaks the division of a massive multi-digit number into an organized, repeating four-step cycle: Divide, Multiply, Subtract, and Bring Down! A popular memory rhyme for these four steps is: Daddy, Mommy, Sister, Brother. Let us trace this cycle by dividing seven thousand and thirty-two by six. Step one, Divide: divide seven by six, which gives one. Step two, Multiply: multiply one by six to get six. Step three, Subtract: subtract six from seven to leave one. Step four, Bring Down: bring down zero from the hundreds place to form ten. Now the cycle repeats! Divide ten by six to get one. Multiply one by six to get six. Subtract six from ten to get four. Bring down three from the tens place to form forty-three. Repeat again! Forty-three divided by six gives seven. Multiply seven by six to get forty-two. Subtract forty-two from forty-three to get one. Finally, bring down two to make twelve; twelve divided by six is two with zero remainder. The quotient is exactly one thousand one hundred and seventy-two. Following the four-step rhythm guarantees complete accuracy.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🔁</span> The 4-Step Long Division Cycle\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Repeat this 4-step sequence for every digit brought down from the dividend:\n                </p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 text-xs font-mono">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 text-center">\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block text-sm">1. DIVIDE (D)</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400 text-[10px]">How many times does it fit?</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 text-center">\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block text-sm">2. MULTIPLY (M)</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400 text-[10px]">Quotient digit × Divisor</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 text-center">\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold block text-sm">3. SUBTRACT (S)</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400 text-[10px]">Find remaining difference</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60 text-center">\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold block text-sm">4. BRING DOWN (B)</span>\n                        <span class="text-slate-400 text-[10px]">Next place value digit</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>📝</span> Vertical Work: 7,032 ÷ 6 = 1,172\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1">\n                        <div class="text-emerald-400 font-bold text-right">Quotient: 1,172</div>\n                        <div class="border-t border-slate-700 pt-1">\n                            <div>6 ) 7 0 3 2</div>\n                            <div class="text-slate-400">- 6 ➔ (7 - 6 = 1)</div>\n                            <div>  1 0 ➔ (bring down 0)</div>\n                            <div class="text-slate-400">-   6 ➔ (10 - 6 = 4)</div>\n                            <div>    4 3 ➔ (bring down 3)</div>\n                            <div class="text-slate-400">-   4 2 ➔ (43 - 42 = 1)</div>\n                            <div>      1 2 ➔ (bring down 2)</div>\n                            <div class="text-emerald-400">-     1 2 ➔ (Remainder = 0)</div>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Memory Rhyme:</strong> <strong>D</strong>ivide, <strong>M</strong>ultiply, <strong>S</strong>ubtract, <strong>B</strong>ring down (Daddy, Mommy, Sister, Brother)!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c12_1', 'question': 'What is the third step in the standard 4-step vertical division algorithm?', 'options': ['Bring Down', 'Subtract', 'Multiply', 'Divide'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Divide (1), Multiply (2), Subtract (3), Bring Down (4).', 'explanation': 'The four steps in order are: 1. Divide, 2. Multiply, 3. Subtract, 4. Bring Down. The third step is Subtract.'}, {'id': 'c12_2', 'question': 'In vertical long division, immediately after you subtract and find the difference, what must you do next?', 'options': ['Multiply by the quotient', 'Bring down the next digit from the dividend', 'Stop the calculation', 'Add the divisor'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'After subtracting, bring down the next place-value digit to form the new working dividend.', 'explanation': 'After subtracting to find the local remainder, the next operation in the cycle is Bring Down.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Zero Quotient Traps', 'tagline': 'Division Using Place Value • Handling Internal Zero Quotients', 'lecture': 'One of the most frequent errors made by primary school students in long division is the famous zero quotient trap! When a student brings down a digit from the dividend, and that new working number is strictly smaller than the divisor, many students mistakenly bring down another digit immediately without writing a zero in the quotient. This omission shrinks the answer by a factor of ten! Consider three thousand and five divided by five. Let us follow the steps carefully: three thousand divided by five gives six in the hundreds place, because six times five is thirty. Thirty minus thirty leaves zero. Now, bring down zero from the tens place. How many times does five go into zero? Exactly zero times! You must write zero in the tens place of the quotient. Next, bring down five from the ones place. Five goes into five exactly one time, giving six hundred and one. If a student forgets the placeholder zero, they write sixty-one instead of six hundred and one! To see how ridiculous sixty-one is, simply multiply back: sixty-one times five is only three hundred and five, not three thousand and five! Never forget: whenever you bring down a digit, you must record a quotient digit, even if that digit is zero.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>⚠️</span> The Fatal Zero-Omission Trap\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Compare the catastrophic error versus the correct place-value division:\n                </p>\n                <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-rose-950/40 border border-rose-800/40">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-rose-300">❌ Fatal Trap: 3,005 ÷ 5 = 61</div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300 mt-1">\n                            The student brought down 0, saw it didn\'t fit, and brought down 5 without writing 0 in the quotient! Verification: 61 × 5 = 305 (WRONG by 2,700!).\n                        </p>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-emerald-300">✓ Correct Method: 3,005 ÷ 5 = 601</div>\n                        <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300 mt-1">\n                            0 tens ÷ 5 = 0 tens in quotient. Verification: 601 × 5 = 3,005 (PERFECT MATCH!).\n                        </p>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🛡️</span> The 1-to-1 Bring Down Rule\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Every single digit brought down from the dividend DEMANDS a digit in the quotient:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1">\n                        <div>4 ) 8 0 2 4</div>\n                        <div>• 8 ÷ 4 = 2 (Hundreds)</div>\n                        <div class="text-amber-300">• Bring down 0 ➔ 0 ÷ 4 = 0 (Tens)</div>\n                        <div class="text-amber-300">• Bring down 2 ➔ 2 ÷ 4 = 0 (Ones)</div>\n                        <div>• Wait: 24 ÷ 4 = 6</div>\n                        <div class="text-emerald-400 font-bold border-t border-slate-700 pt-1">Result: 8,024 ÷ 4 = 2,006</div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-cyan-950/40 border border-cyan-800/40 text-[11px] text-cyan-200 mt-3">\n                    🔍 <strong>Quick Sanity Check:</strong> 8,000 ÷ 4 is roughly 2,000. So the quotient must have four digits (around 2,000), never three!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c13_1', 'question': 'What is the quotient when 4,016 is divided by 4?', 'options': ['1,004', '104', '1,040', '14'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '4 ÷ 4 = 1. Bring down 0 ➔ 0. Bring down 1 ➔ 0. Bring down 6 ➔ 16 ÷ 4 = 4.', 'explanation': '4,016 ÷ 4: 4 ÷ 4 = 1; 0 ÷ 4 = 0; 1 ÷ 4 = 0; 16 ÷ 4 = 4. Quotient = 1,004. Notice the two placeholder zeros!'}, {'id': 'c13_2', 'question': 'A student calculates 6,036 ÷ 6 and writes 16. Why is this answer clearly incorrect?', 'options': ['Because 16 × 6 = 96, which is nowhere near 6,036!', 'Because 6,036 is an odd number', 'Because 6 cannot divide 36', 'Because 6,000 ÷ 6 is 100'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': "Multiply the student's answer by the divisor to check reasonableness.", 'explanation': '6,000 ÷ 6 is 1,000, so the quotient must be around 1,006. 16 is smaller by a factor of over 60 times because the student dropped two zeros!'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Resource Bottlenecks', 'tagline': 'Resource Optimization & Tour Logistics • Identifying Limiting Constraints', 'lecture': 'In modern engineering, furniture manufacturing, and sports tour planning, multi-step division is used to identify bottlenecks—the single limiting resource that caps total production. Consider a village carpenter who manufactures wooden bookshelves for rural school libraries. Each bookshelf requires four wooden panels, six joining clips, and sixteen steel screws. In his workshop inventory, the carpenter counts twenty-six panels, thirty-three clips, and eighty screws. How many complete bookshelves can he build? Let us analyze each component separately using division. For panels: twenty-six divided by four equals six bookshelves with a remainder of two panels. For clips: thirty-three divided by six equals five bookshelves with a remainder of three clips. For screws: eighty divided by sixteen equals exactly five bookshelves! Comparing the three division results: the panels allow six shelves, but the clips and screws allow only five. Even though there are two leftover panels, the carpenter cannot build a sixth shelf because he has completely run out of clips and screws! The limiting resources are the clips and screws, restricting production to five complete bookshelves. Likewise, on a five hundred and eighty-five kilometre cycle rally, nine equal daily stages require riding sixty-five kilometres per day. Identifying bottlenecks through division prevents over-ordering redundant parts and optimizes commercial workshop productivity.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🪚</span> The Carpenter\'s Stock Bottleneck\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Each bookshelf needs: 4 panels, 6 clips, 16 screws:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Panels (26 in stock):</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300">26 ÷ 4 = <strong>6 Shelves</strong> (R 2)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Clips (33 in stock):</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300">33 ÷ 6 = <strong>5 Shelves</strong> (R 3)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Screws (80 in stock):</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-300">80 ÷ 16 = <strong>5 Shelves</strong> (R 0)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Max Bookshelves Built:</span>\n                        <span>5 Shelves (Bottleneck = Clips & Screws)</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🚴</span> Mumbai to Goa Cycle Rally Route\n                    </h4>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">\n                        Planning 585 km route via Ratnagiri over uniform daily riding stages:\n                    </p>\n                    <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Total Distance:</span>\n                            <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">585 km</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                            <span>Stage Plan:</span>\n                            <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold">9 Riding Days</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                            <span>Daily Stage Target:</span>\n                            <span>585 ÷ 9 = 65 km/day</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Bottleneck Principle:</strong> The overall capacity of any manufacturing system is determined by its most scarce component—the minimum of all quotient limits!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c14_1', 'question': 'A toy car factory requires 4 wheels and 2 seats to assemble 1 toy car. If their stock contains 50 wheels and 22 seats, what is the maximum number of toy cars they can complete?', 'options': ['11 cars', '12 cars', '10 cars', '22 cars'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Wheels allow: 50 ÷ 4 = 12 cars. Seats allow: 22 ÷ 2 = 11 cars. Take the smaller limit.', 'explanation': '50 wheels can make 12 cars (with 2 wheels left). But 22 seats can make only 11 cars. When 11 cars are built, all seats are exhausted. Max cars = 11.'}, {'id': 'c14_2', 'question': 'A coastal cycle rally of 672 km is scheduled across 8 equal daily stages. What distance must riders complete each day?', 'options': ['84 km', '82 km', '86 km', '74 km'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide 672 by 8: 67 ÷ 8 = 8 R 3; 32 ÷ 8 = 4.', 'explanation': '672 ÷ 8 = 84 km per day.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Market Invoices', 'tagline': 'Vegetable Market • Record Keeping & Unit Rate Calculation', 'lecture': 'At a bustling rural sabzi mandi or agricultural produce market, farmers sell harvested vegetables in bulk crates and maintain transparent accounting ledgers. The fundamental business formula governing every authentic commercial invoice connects three key quantities: Total Cost equals Rate per Kilogram multiplied by Quantity in Kilograms. Whenever a column in the ledger is missing, division provides the exact missing value! If a vegetable vendor sells seventy-two kilograms of fresh farm potatoes for one thousand four hundred and forty rupees, what was the rate per kilogram? Dividing one thousand four hundred and forty by seventy-two yields twenty rupees per kilogram. Conversely, if a restaurant chef spends eight hundred and seventy-five rupees on fresh green peas sold at thirty-five rupees per kilogram, how many kilograms of green peas did the chef purchase? We divide eight hundred and seventy-five by thirty-five. By estimating eighty-seven divided by thirty-five, which gives two with a remainder of seventeen, and bringing down five to make one hundred and seventy-five, thirty-five times five is one hundred and seventy-five. The chef bought exactly twenty-five kilograms of green peas! Every farmer checks their records carefully to prevent accounting errors and maintain honest trade across markets. Auditing farm invoice tables builds practical financial literacy and sharpens everyday mental arithmetic.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>🥦</span> Sabzi Mandi Sales Ledger\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Use division to find missing rates or quantities: Rate = Total ÷ Quantity:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="grid grid-cols-4 text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-700 pb-1 text-[11px]">\n                        <span>ITEM</span>\n                        <span class="text-right">WEIGHT</span>\n                        <span class="text-right">RATE/KG</span>\n                        <span class="text-right">TOTAL</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="grid grid-cols-4 text-slate-200 py-1">\n                        <span>🥔 Potato</span>\n                        <span class="text-right">72 kg</span>\n                        <span class="text-right text-cyan-300 font-bold">₹20</span>\n                        <span class="text-right">₹1,440</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="grid grid-cols-4 text-slate-200 py-1 border-b border-slate-700">\n                        <span>🌱 Green Peas</span>\n                        <span class="text-right text-cyan-300 font-bold">25 kg</span>\n                        <span class="text-right">₹35</span>\n                        <span class="text-right">₹875</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-xs">\n                        <span>Combined Sales:</span>\n                        <span>₹2,315</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>📐</span> The Commercial Invoice Formulas\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="space-y-2 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                            <span class="font-bold text-amber-300">To Find Rate per Kilogram:</span>\n                            <div class="font-mono text-cyan-300 mt-0.5">Rate = Total Cost ÷ Weight (kg)</div>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-700/60">\n                            <span class="font-bold text-amber-300">To Find Weight Purchased:</span>\n                            <div class="font-mono text-cyan-300 mt-0.5">Weight (kg) = Total Cost ÷ Rate per kg</div>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40 text-[11px] text-emerald-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Verification:</strong> Rate × Quantity MUST equal Total Amount on every authentic grocery invoice!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c15_1', 'question': 'A vegetable vendor sells 48 kg of cabbages and earns ₹1,200. What was the unit price per kilogram of cabbage?', 'options': ['₹25 / kg', '₹24 / kg', '₹20 / kg', '₹30 / kg'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide ₹1,200 by 48 kg: 1,200 ÷ 48.', 'explanation': 'Rate per kg = Total ÷ Weight = 1,200 ÷ 48 = ₹25 per kg.'}, {'id': 'c15_2', 'question': 'Radishes are priced at ₹18 per kg. If a hotel chef spends ₹648 on radishes, how many kilograms were bought?', 'options': ['36 kg', '34 kg', '38 kg', '32 kg'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide ₹648 by 18: 64 ÷ 18 = 3 R 10; 108 ÷ 18 = 6.', 'explanation': 'Weight = 648 ÷ 18 = 36 kg.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Mathematical Truth', 'tagline': 'Mathematical Statements • Equality Balance & Truth Value Classification', 'lecture': 'In mathematics, an equation represents an exact and unyielding balance of numerical values across the equal sign. The equal sign does not simply mean calculate the answer; it acts as a balanced weighing beam declaring that the total value on the left-hand side is identically equal to the value on the right-hand side. Consider the statement: eight multiplied by nine equals seventy plus two. Let us evaluate both sides independently: eight times nine is seventy-two. On the right side, seventy plus two is also seventy-two. Because seventy-two equals seventy-two, the statement is declared mathematically True! Beyond simple arithmetic equations, mathematicians classify general mathematical statements into three categories: Always True, Sometimes True, and Never True. For example, the statement: dividing an even number by two produces an exact integer with zero remainder is Always True, because that is the very definition of an even number! In contrast, the statement: dividing an even number by two produces an odd number is Sometimes True—for instance, six divided by two is three, which is odd, but eight divided by two is four, which is even. Testing open equations and discovering single counterexamples enables mathematicians to establish universal truth across geometry and arithmetic. Rigorous evaluation of truth values develops sharp critical thinking.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                    <span>⚖️</span> Equation Equality Balance\n                </h4>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">\n                    Evaluate both sides independently to verify mathematical truth:\n                </p>\n                <div class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-700/80 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">\n                    <div class="text-center font-bold text-amber-300 text-sm">8 × 9 = 70 + 2</div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300 border-t border-slate-700 pt-1">\n                        <span>Left Side (LHS):</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">8 × 9 = 72</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-300">\n                        <span>Right Side (RHS):</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-300 font-bold">70 + 2 = 72</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between border-t border-slate-700 pt-1 text-emerald-400 font-black text-sm">\n                        <span>Verdict: LHS = RHS</span>\n                        <span>✅ TRUE STATEMENT</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="bg-slate-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 flex flex-col justify-between">\n                <div>\n                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">\n                        <span>🧭</span> Truth Value Classification\n                    </h4>\n                    <div class="space-y-1.5 text-xs">\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-800/40">\n                            <span class="font-bold text-emerald-300">Always True:</span>\n                            <div class="text-slate-300 text-[11px]">"Any even number divided by 2 leaves remainder 0."</div>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-800/40">\n                            <span class="font-bold text-amber-300">Sometimes True:</span>\n                            <div class="text-slate-300 text-[11px]">"Dividing an even number by 2 gives an odd quotient." (6÷2=3 Odd, but 8÷2=4 Even!)</div>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-rose-950/40 border border-rose-800/40">\n                            <span class="font-bold text-rose-300">Never True:</span>\n                            <div class="text-slate-300 text-[11px]">"A remainder can be strictly greater than its divisor." (Defies division law!)</div>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-800/40 text-[11px] text-indigo-200 mt-3">\n                    💡 <strong>Counterexample Rule:</strong> To prove a statement is NOT always true, you only need to discover a single counterexample!\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'id': 'c16_1', 'question': 'Which of the following equations represents a TRUE mathematical statement?', 'options': ['6 × 7 = 40 + 2', '9 × 4 = 30 + 5', '8 × 8 = 60 + 5', '7 × 5 = 40 - 2'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Calculate 6 × 7 and compare with 40 + 2.', 'explanation': '6 × 7 = 42, and 40 + 2 = 42. Since 42 = 42, it is a True statement. In B, 36 ≠ 35. In C, 64 ≠ 65. In D, 35 ≠ 38.'}, {'id': 'c16_2', 'question': "Classify the statement: 'When a number ending in 0 is divided by 5, the remainder is 0.'", 'options': ['Always True', 'Sometimes True', 'Never True', 'Cannot be determined'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think of multiples of 5: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30. Any number ending in 0 is a multiple of 10, hence divisible by 5.', 'explanation': 'Every number ending in 0 is divisible by 10, and since 10 is a multiple of 5, every number ending in 0 is always exactly divisible by 5 with remainder 0. It is Always True.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'title': 'Challenge 1', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 1 • Foundations, Fact Triangles, Zero Patterns & Splitting', 'lecture': 'Welcome to Mastery Challenge 1. In this interactive session, you will test your understanding of division as the inverse of multiplication, number fact triangles, zero cancellation shortcuts, and mental dividend splitting.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Test your mastery of foundational division facts, place value patterns, and mental splitting strategies. Answer all 10 questions to unlock detailed step-by-step solutions!</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q1', 'question': 'In the mathematical equation 96 ÷ 8 = 12, what is the specific mathematical term for the number 8?', 'options': ['Dividend', 'Divisor', 'Quotient', 'Remainder'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '8 is the number of equal groups into which the total is being partitioned.', 'explanation': 'In 96 ÷ 8 = 12, 96 is the Dividend (N), 8 is the Divisor (D), and 12 is the Quotient (Q).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-200'>\n                        <strong>Division Terminology Breakdown:</strong>\n                        <ul class='list-disc list-inside mt-1 space-y-1 text-slate-300'>\n                            <li><strong>96 (Dividend, N):</strong> The total quantity to be divided.</li>\n                            <li><strong>8 (Divisor, D):</strong> The number of equal groups dividing the total.</li>\n                            <li><strong>12 (Quotient, Q):</strong> The size or result of each equal part.</li>\n                        </ul>\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: Divisor</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q2', 'question': 'From the multiplication fact 40 × 50 = 2,000, which of the following is an authentic derived division fact?', 'options': ['2,000 ÷ 40 = 50', '2,000 ÷ 20 = 100', '2,000 ÷ 5 = 40', '40 ÷ 2,000 = 50'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide the product (2,000) by one factor (40) to obtain the other factor (50).', 'explanation': 'Since 40 × 50 = 2,000, dividing 2,000 by 40 yields 50, and dividing 2,000 by 50 yields 40.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Multiplication: 40 × 50 = 2,000<br>\n                        Derived Fact 1: 2,000 ÷ 40 = 50<br>\n                        Derived Fact 2: 2,000 ÷ 50 = 40\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 2,000 ÷ 40 = 50</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q3', 'question': 'In a number fact triangle, the top product box is 540, and the bottom-right node is 60. What number belongs in the bottom-left node?', 'options': ['9', '90', '8', '6'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide 540 by 60 by canceling one zero.', 'explanation': '540 ÷ 60 = 54 ÷ 6 = 9. So the missing factor is 9.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Peak = 540<br>\n                        Known Factor = 60<br>\n                        Missing Factor = 540 ÷ 60 = 54 ÷ 6 = 9\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 9</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q4', 'question': 'Evaluate 64,000 ÷ 80 using the trailing zero cancellation shortcut:', 'options': ['80', '800', '8,000', '8'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Cancel 1 zero from both 64,000 and 80: 6,400 ÷ 8.', 'explanation': '64,000 ÷ 80: Cancel one zero from each side to get 6,400 ÷ 8. Since 64 ÷ 8 = 8, 6,400 ÷ 8 = 800.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        64,000 ÷ 80<br>\n                        = (64,000 ÷ 10) ÷ (80 ÷ 10)<br>\n                        = 6,400 ÷ 8 = 800\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 800</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q5', 'question': 'A fruit trader packs 3,600 oranges equally into crates of 90 oranges each. How many crates are filled?', 'options': ['4 crates', '40 crates', '400 crates', '45 crates'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Cancel one zero: 360 ÷ 9 = 40.', 'explanation': '3,600 ÷ 90 = 360 ÷ 9 = 40 crates.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Total Oranges = 3,600<br>\n                        Per Crate = 90<br>\n                        Crates = 3,600 ÷ 90 = 360 ÷ 9 = 40 crates\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 40 crates</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q6', 'question': 'Using convenient additive splitting, what is the best mental decomposition to solve 1,854 ÷ 6?', 'options': ['(1,800 ÷ 6) + (54 ÷ 6) = 300 + 9 = 309', '(1,000 ÷ 6) + (854 ÷ 6) = 166 + 143', '(1,800 ÷ 6) + (54 ÷ 6) = 30 + 9 = 39', '(1,200 ÷ 6) + (654 ÷ 6) = 200 + 109 = 309'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '1,854 splits into 1,800 and 54, both clean multiples of 6.', 'explanation': '1,854 = 1,800 + 54. 1,800 ÷ 6 = 300, and 54 ÷ 6 = 9. Combining gives 300 + 9 = 309.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1,854 ÷ 6 = (1,800 ÷ 6) + (54 ÷ 6)<br>\n                        = 300 + 9 = 309\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: (1,800 ÷ 6) + (54 ÷ 6) = 300 + 9 = 309</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q7', 'question': 'Use subtractive convenient splitting to calculate 3,984 ÷ 4 mentally:', 'options': ['(4,000 ÷ 4) - (16 ÷ 4) = 1,000 - 4 = 996', '(4,000 ÷ 4) - (14 ÷ 4) = 1,000 - 3.5 = 996.5', '(3,600 ÷ 4) + (384 ÷ 4) = 900 + 96 = 996', '(4,000 ÷ 4) - (16 ÷ 4) = 100 - 4 = 96'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '3,984 is 16 less than 4,000. Write as (4,000 - 16) ÷ 4.', 'explanation': '3,984 = 4,000 - 16. (4,000 ÷ 4) - (16 ÷ 4) = 1,000 - 4 = 996.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        3,984 = 4,000 - 16<br>\n                        3,984 ÷ 4 = (4,000 ÷ 4) - (16 ÷ 4)<br>\n                        = 1,000 - 4 = 996\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: (4,000 ÷ 4) - (16 ÷ 4) = 1,000 - 4 = 996</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q8', 'question': "A courier delivery driver travels 240 km in 6 hours at a constant speed. What is the driver's speed per hour?", 'options': ['40 km/h', '30 km/h', '50 km/h', '45 km/h'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Speed = Distance ÷ Time = 240 ÷ 6.', 'explanation': '240 ÷ 6 = 40 km/h.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Speed = Distance ÷ Time<br>\n                        = 240 km ÷ 6 h = 40 km/h\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 40 km/h</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q9', 'question': 'Which of the following is equivalent to 8,100 ÷ 90?', 'options': ['9', '90', '900', '810'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Cancel one zero: 810 ÷ 9 = 90.', 'explanation': '8,100 ÷ 90 = 810 ÷ 9 = 90.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        8,100 ÷ 90 = 810 ÷ 9 = 90\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 90</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q10', 'question': 'A dairy cooperative packs 1,200 litres of milk into 20-litre cans. How many cans are filled?', 'options': ['60 cans', '6 cans', '600 cans', '50 cans'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Cancel 1 zero: 120 ÷ 2 = 60.', 'explanation': '1,200 ÷ 20 = 120 ÷ 2 = 60 cans.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        1,200 ÷ 20 = 120 ÷ 2 = 60 cans\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 60 cans</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}, {'id': 18, 'title': 'Challenge 2', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 2 • Halving, Partial Quotients & The Remainder Algorithm', 'lecture': 'Welcome to Mastery Challenge 2. In this module, you will practice repeated halving, multi-step partial quotient algorithms, and the Fundamental Division Algorithm with remainders.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Test your mastery of powers-of-two halving, partial quotient leaps, and remainder constraints. Answer all 10 questions to unlock detailed solutions!</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q11', 'question': 'Use repeated halving to divide 672 by 4:', 'options': ['168', '164', '172', '336'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Half of 672 is 336. Half of 336 is 168.', 'explanation': '672 ÷ 2 = 336. 336 ÷ 2 = 168. So 672 ÷ 4 = 168.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        First Half: 672 ÷ 2 = 336<br>\n                        Second Half: 336 ÷ 2 = 168<br>\n                        Result: 672 ÷ 4 = 168\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 168</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q12', 'question': 'What is 1,536 ÷ 8 using three successive halves?', 'options': ['192', '182', '384', '768'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '1,536 ÷ 2 = 768. 768 ÷ 2 = 384. 384 ÷ 2 = 192.', 'explanation': 'First half: 768. Second half: 384. Third half: 192. So 1,536 ÷ 8 = 192.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Half 1: 1,536 ÷ 2 = 768<br>\n                        Half 2: 768 ÷ 2 = 384<br>\n                        Half 3: 384 ÷ 2 = 192<br>\n                        Result: 1,536 ÷ 8 = 192\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 192</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q13', 'question': "In Sunitha's partial quotient method for 738 ÷ 6, she first subtracts 100 × 6 = 600. After this first leap, what is the remaining quantity to divide?", 'options': ['138', '128', '238', '38'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Subtract 600 from 738.', 'explanation': '738 - 600 = 138. 138 remains to be divided by 6.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        738 - (100 × 6) = 738 - 600 = 138<br>\n                        Next Leap: 20 × 6 = 120 (leaves 18)<br>\n                        Final Leap: 3 × 6 = 18 (leaves 0)<br>\n                        Total Q = 100 + 20 + 3 = 123\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 138</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q14', 'question': 'What is the complete quotient of 738 ÷ 6?', 'options': ['123', '124', '113', '133'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '100 + 20 + 3 = 123.', 'explanation': '738 ÷ 6 = 123. Verification: 123 × 6 = 738.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        738 ÷ 6 = 123<br>\n                        Check: 123 × 6 = 738\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 123</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q15', 'question': 'If Divisor = 7, Quotient = 24, and Remainder = 5, what is the Dividend (N)?', 'options': ['173', '168', '175', '163'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'N = (D × Q) + R = (7 × 24) + 5.', 'explanation': '7 × 24 = 168. 168 + 5 = 173. So N = 173.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        N = (D × Q) + R<br>\n                        = (7 × 24) + 5<br>\n                        = 168 + 5 = 173\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 173</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q16', 'question': 'Which of the following could BE a remainder when dividing an integer by 9?', 'options': ['8', '9', '10', '12'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'The remainder must satisfy 0 ≤ R < 9.', 'explanation': 'Because 0 ≤ R < D, dividing by 9 can only give remainders 0 through 8. 8 is valid; 9, 10, and 12 are impossible.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-200'>\n                        Condition: 0 ≤ R < Divisor<br>\n                        For Divisor = 9, possible remainders are {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8}.<br>\n                        Therefore, 8 is the only valid option.\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 8</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q17', 'question': 'A school has 312 students going on a zoo trip. Each bus seats 45 students. How many buses must be booked so no student is left behind?', 'options': ['6 buses', '7 buses', '8 buses', '5 buses'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '312 ÷ 45 = 6 with a remainder of 42 students.', 'explanation': '312 ÷ 45 = 6 R 42. 6 buses only hold 270 students, leaving 42 students behind. A 7th bus must be booked for the remainder.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        312 ÷ 45 = 6 with remainder 42<br>\n                        6 buses × 45 = 270 seats (42 students left out!)<br>\n                        Must round UP: 6 + 1 = 7 buses\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 7 buses</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q18', 'question': 'A baker bakes 250 muffins and packs them into boxes holding 6 muffins each. How many boxes are completely filled, and how many muffins are left over?', 'options': ['41 boxes filled, 4 muffins left over', '40 boxes filled, 10 muffins left over', '42 boxes filled, 2 muffins left over', '39 boxes filled, 16 muffins left over'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '250 ÷ 6 = 41 with a remainder of 4.', 'explanation': '250 ÷ 6 = 41 with remainder 4. (41 × 6 = 246; 250 - 246 = 4).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        250 ÷ 6 = 41 R 4<br>\n                        Verification: (41 × 6) + 4 = 246 + 4 = 250\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 41 boxes filled, 4 muffins left over</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q19', 'question': 'A farmer has 195 eggs. Egg cartons hold 12 eggs each. How many cartons can be completely filled?', 'options': ['16 cartons', '15 cartons', '17 cartons', '14 cartons'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '195 ÷ 12 = 16 R 3.', 'explanation': '195 ÷ 12 = 16 with a remainder of 3 eggs. Exactly 16 cartons are completely filled.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        195 ÷ 12 = 16 R 3<br>\n                        16 × 12 = 192 eggs in full cartons.<br>\n                        3 eggs remain in an incomplete carton.\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 16 cartons</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q20', 'question': 'If a number is divided by 11, the quotient is 18 and the remainder is 7. What is the number?', 'options': ['205', '198', '208', '195'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'N = (11 × 18) + 7.', 'explanation': '11 × 18 = 198. 198 + 7 = 205.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        N = (D × Q) + R<br>\n                        = (11 × 18) + 7<br>\n                        = 198 + 7 = 205\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 205</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}, {'id': 19, 'title': 'Challenge 3', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 3 • Bulk Calculations, Multi-Digit Divisors & Zero Traps', 'lecture': 'Welcome to Mastery Challenge 3. In this assessment, you will master multi-digit vertical division, identify and eliminate internal zero quotient traps, and calculate bulk farm oil extraction yields.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Test your mastery of multi-digit divisors, quotient placeholders, and agricultural processing mathematics. Answer all 10 questions to unlock detailed solutions!</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q21', 'question': 'An oil mill extracts 1 litre of coconut oil from every 8 coconuts. How many litres of oil are produced from 5,128 coconuts?', 'options': ['641 Litres', '640 Litres', '651 Litres', '631 Litres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '51 ÷ 8 = 6 R 3; 32 ÷ 8 = 4 R 0; 8 ÷ 8 = 1.', 'explanation': '5,128 ÷ 8 = 641 litres of oil.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        5,128 ÷ 8<br>\n                        • 51 ÷ 8 = 6 (R 3)<br>\n                        • 32 ÷ 8 = 4 (R 0)<br>\n                        • 8 ÷ 8 = 1 (R 0)<br>\n                        = 641 Litres\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 641 Litres</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q22', 'question': 'If 641 litres of coconut oil is sold at ₹250 per litre, what is the total revenue?', 'options': ['₹1,60,250', '₹1,60,000', '₹1,58,250', '₹1,62,500'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '641 × 250 = (641 × 1,000) ÷ 4 = 641,000 ÷ 4.', 'explanation': '641 × ₹250 = ₹1,60,250.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Revenue = 641 × ₹250<br>\n                        = 641 × (1,000 ÷ 4)<br>\n                        = 641,000 ÷ 4 = ₹1,60,250\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: ₹1,60,250</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q23', 'question': 'What is the quotient of 9,805 ÷ 32?', 'options': ['306 with remainder 13', '36 with remainder 13', '360 with remainder 13', '305 with remainder 15'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '98 ÷ 32 = 3 R 2; bring down 0 ➔ 20 ÷ 32 = 0; bring down 5 ➔ 205 ÷ 32 = 6 R 13.', 'explanation': '98 ÷ 32 = 3 R 2. Bring down 0: 20 ÷ 32 = 0 R 20. Bring down 5: 205 ÷ 32 = 6 R 13. Quotient = 306, Remainder = 13.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        9,805 ÷ 32<br>\n                        • 98 ÷ 32 = 3 (3 × 32 = 96; Rem = 2)<br>\n                        • Bring down 0 ➔ 20 ÷ 32 = 0 (MUST WRITE 0!)<br>\n                        • Bring down 5 ➔ 205 ÷ 32 = 6 (6 × 32 = 192; Rem = 13)<br>\n                        Result = 306 R 13\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 306 with remainder 13</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q24', 'question': 'Calculate 6,018 ÷ 6. Beware of the zero quotient trap!', 'options': ['1,003', '103', '1,030', '13'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '6 ÷ 6 = 1; 0 ÷ 6 = 0; 1 ÷ 6 = 0; 18 ÷ 6 = 3.', 'explanation': '6 ÷ 6 = 1. Bring down 0: 0 ÷ 6 = 0. Bring down 1: 1 ÷ 6 = 0. Bring down 8: 18 ÷ 6 = 3. Quotient = 1,003.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        6,018 ÷ 6<br>\n                        6 ÷ 6 = 1 (Thousands)<br>\n                        0 ÷ 6 = 0 (Hundreds)<br>\n                        1 ÷ 6 = 0 (Tens)<br>\n                        18 ÷ 6 = 3 (Ones)<br>\n                        Quotient = 1,003\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 1,003</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q25', 'question': 'A worker presses 8,625 kg of coir fiber into bales of 25 kg each. How many bales are pressed?', 'options': ['345 bales', '335 bales', '355 bales', '325 bales'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '86 ÷ 25 = 3 R 11; 112 ÷ 25 = 4 R 12; 125 ÷ 25 = 5.', 'explanation': '8,625 ÷ 25 = 345 bales.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        8,625 ÷ 25<br>\n                        • 86 ÷ 25 = 3 (3 × 25 = 75; Rem = 11)<br>\n                        • 112 ÷ 25 = 4 (4 × 25 = 100; Rem = 12)<br>\n                        • 125 ÷ 25 = 5 (5 × 25 = 125; Rem = 0)<br>\n                        Total = 345 Bales\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 345 bales</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q26', 'question': 'Evaluate 2,874 ÷ 14:', 'options': ['205 with remainder 4', '25 with remainder 4', '204 with remainder 18', '215 with remainder 4'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '28 ÷ 14 = 2; bring down 7 ➔ 7 ÷ 14 = 0; bring down 4 ➔ 74 ÷ 14 = 5 R 4.', 'explanation': '28 ÷ 14 = 2. Bring down 7: 7 ÷ 14 = 0. Bring down 4: 74 ÷ 14 = 5 R 4. Result = 205 R 4.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        2,874 ÷ 14<br>\n                        • 28 ÷ 14 = 2 (Rem = 0)<br>\n                        • Bring down 7 ➔ 7 ÷ 14 = 0 (Rem = 7)<br>\n                        • Bring down 4 ➔ 74 ÷ 14 = 5 (5 × 14 = 70; Rem = 4)<br>\n                        Result = 205 R 4\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 205 with remainder 4</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q27', 'question': 'A theater hall has 576 seats arranged in 18 equal rows. How many seats are in each row?', 'options': ['32 seats', '34 seats', '28 seats', '36 seats'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '57 ÷ 18 = 3 R 3; 36 ÷ 18 = 2.', 'explanation': '576 ÷ 18 = 32 seats per row.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        576 ÷ 18<br>\n                        57 ÷ 18 = 3 (3 × 18 = 54; Rem = 3)<br>\n                        36 ÷ 18 = 2 (2 × 18 = 36; Rem = 0)<br>\n                        = 32 seats\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 32 seats</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q28', 'question': 'What is 7,248 ÷ 12?', 'options': ['604', '64', '640', '608'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '72 ÷ 12 = 6; 4 ÷ 12 = 0; 48 ÷ 12 = 4.', 'explanation': '72 ÷ 12 = 6. Bring down 4: 4 ÷ 12 = 0. Bring down 8: 48 ÷ 12 = 4. Quotient = 604.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        7,248 ÷ 12 = 604<br>\n                        Check: 604 × 12 = 7,248\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 604</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q29', 'question': 'A warehouse receives 3,750 bags of sugar and distributes them equally among 15 supermarkets. How many bags does each supermarket receive?', 'options': ['250 bags', '25 bags', '205 bags', '260 bags'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '37 ÷ 15 = 2 R 7; 75 ÷ 15 = 5; 0 ÷ 15 = 0.', 'explanation': '3,750 ÷ 15 = 250 bags.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        3,750 ÷ 15 = 250 bags\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 250 bags</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q30', 'question': 'If 8,000 ÷ 16 = 500, what is 8,000 ÷ 32?', 'options': ['250', '1,000', '125', '500'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Doubling the divisor cuts the quotient in half!', 'explanation': 'Since 32 is twice 16, dividing by 32 yields half of 500, which is 250.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Divisor doubles: 16 × 2 = 32<br>\n                        Quotient halves: 500 ÷ 2 = 250<br>\n                        Result: 8,000 ÷ 32 = 250\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 250</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}, {'id': 20, 'title': 'Challenge 4', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 4 • Invoices, Bottlenecks, Tour Planning & Mathematical Truth', 'lecture': 'Welcome to the final Mastery Challenge of Chapter 9. This concluding assessment challenges you with sabzi mandi invoices, workshop bottleneck optimization, cycle rally route planning, and truth value classification.', 'content_html': "<p class='text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4'>Congratulations on reaching the final mastery challenge! Solve these 10 multi-step optimization and truth-value problems to complete the Coconut Farm chapter.</p>", 'questions': [{'id': 'q31', 'question': 'A sabzi mandi invoice records that 65 kg of onions were sold for ₹1,625. What was the unit price per kilogram?', 'options': ['₹25 / kg', '₹24 / kg', '₹26 / kg', '₹28 / kg'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Rate = Total ÷ Weight = 1,625 ÷ 65.', 'explanation': '1,625 ÷ 65 = 25 rupees per kg.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Rate = Total Cost ÷ Weight<br>\n                        = ₹1,625 ÷ 65 kg = ₹25 / kg\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: ₹25 / kg</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q32', 'question': 'Tomatoes sell at ₹32 per kg. A restaurant chef purchases ₹1,440 worth of tomatoes. How many kilograms were purchased?', 'options': ['45 kg', '42 kg', '40 kg', '48 kg'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Weight = Total ÷ Rate = 1,440 ÷ 32.', 'explanation': '1,440 ÷ 32 = 45 kg.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Weight = Total Cost ÷ Rate<br>\n                        = ₹1,440 ÷ ₹32 = 45 kg\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 45 kg</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q33', 'question': 'A bicycle assembly workshop has 75 frames, 160 wheels, and 90 handlebars. Each bicycle requires 1 frame, 2 wheels, and 1 handlebar. What is the maximum number of bicycles that can be assembled?', 'options': ['75 bicycles', '80 bicycles', '90 bicycles', '70 bicycles'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Frames allow: 75. Wheels allow: 160 ÷ 2 = 80. Handlebars allow: 90. Find the minimum limit.', 'explanation': 'Frames allow 75 bikes. Wheels allow 80 bikes. Handlebars allow 90 bikes. The bottleneck is the frames (75), which caps production at 75 bicycles.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        • Frames: 75 ÷ 1 = 75 bikes (Bottleneck!)<br>\n                        • Wheels: 160 ÷ 2 = 80 bikes<br>\n                        • Handlebars: 90 ÷ 1 = 90 bikes<br>\n                        Max Complete Bikes = 75\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 75 bicycles</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q34', 'question': 'A coastal cycling expedition covers 702 km from Mumbai to Goa across 9 equal daily stages. What distance must the cyclists ride each day?', 'options': ['78 km', '76 km', '74 km', '82 km'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '70 ÷ 9 = 7 R 7; 72 ÷ 9 = 8.', 'explanation': '702 ÷ 9 = 78 km per day.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        Daily Stage = 702 km ÷ 9 days = 78 km/day\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 78 km</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q35', 'question': 'Which of the following equations represents a TRUE mathematical statement?', 'options': ['7 × 8 = 50 + 6', '9 × 7 = 60 + 5', '8 × 6 = 40 + 7', '6 × 6 = 30 + 5'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '7 × 8 = 56, and 50 + 6 = 56.', 'explanation': '7 × 8 = 56, and 50 + 6 = 56. 56 = 56, which is True.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        LHS = 7 × 8 = 56<br>\n                        RHS = 50 + 6 = 56<br>\n                        LHS = RHS ➔ TRUE!\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 7 × 8 = 50 + 6</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q36', 'question': "Classify the statement: 'When an odd number is divided by 2, the remainder is 1.'", 'options': ['Always True', 'Sometimes True', 'Never True', 'Cannot be determined'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Test odd numbers: 3 ÷ 2 = 1 R 1; 5 ÷ 2 = 2 R 1; 7 ÷ 2 = 3 R 1.', 'explanation': 'By definition, an odd number is of the form 2k + 1. Dividing any odd number by 2 always leaves a remainder of exactly 1. It is Always True.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-200'>\n                        All odd numbers leave remainder 1 when divided by 2.<br>\n                        Examples: 3 = (2 × 1) + 1, 15 = (2 × 7) + 1, 99 = (2 × 49) + 1.<br>\n                        This is the universal definition of odd integers.\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: Always True</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q37', 'question': "Classify the statement: 'Dividing a number by 4 gives an even quotient.'", 'options': ['Sometimes True', 'Always True', 'Never True', 'Cannot be determined'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Try 16 ÷ 4 = 4 (Even), but 12 ÷ 4 = 3 (Odd!).', 'explanation': '16 ÷ 4 = 4 (even quotient), but 12 ÷ 4 = 3 (odd quotient). Because it is true for some numbers and false for others, it is Sometimes True.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-200'>\n                        • Case 1: 16 ÷ 4 = 4 (Even ➔ True)<br>\n                        • Case 2: 12 ÷ 4 = 3 (Odd ➔ False)<br>\n                        Since it depends on the dividend, the statement is <strong>Sometimes True</strong>.\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: Sometimes True</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q38', 'question': "Classify the statement: 'A number divided by 10 has a remainder of 15.'", 'options': ['Never True', 'Always True', 'Sometimes True', 'Cannot be determined'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'The remainder must be strictly smaller than the divisor (R < 10).', 'explanation': 'In division by 10, the remainder can only be 0 through 9. A remainder of 15 is mathematically impossible under the division algorithm. It is Never True.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 text-slate-200'>\n                        Condition: 0 ≤ R < Divisor<br>\n                        When Divisor = 10, remainder must be < 10.<br>\n                        A remainder of 15 violates the fundamental division algorithm.\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: Never True</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q39', 'question': 'A school buys 450 ice cream cups for sports day. If they are packed in cartons of 24 cups each, how many full cartons are bought, and how many extra loose cups remain?', 'options': ['18 cartons with 18 cups remaining', '18 cartons with 12 cups remaining', '19 cartons with 6 cups remaining', '17 cartons with 24 cups remaining'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '450 ÷ 24 = 18 with a remainder of 18.', 'explanation': '450 ÷ 24 = 18 with remainder 18. (18 × 24 = 432; 450 - 432 = 18).', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        450 ÷ 24<br>\n                        45 ÷ 24 = 1 R 21<br>\n                        210 ÷ 24 = 8 R 18<br>\n                        = 18 cartons and 18 extra cups\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 18 cartons with 18 cups remaining</p>\n                </div>\n                "}, {'id': 'q40', 'question': 'In a factory warehouse, 5,000 biscuit packets are loaded equally into 25 shipping crates. How many packets are in each crate?', 'options': ['200 packets', '20 packets', '250 packets', '500 packets'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '50 ÷ 25 = 2. Append two zeros.', 'explanation': '5,000 ÷ 25 = 200 packets.', 'solution_html': "\n                <div class='space-y-2 text-xs'>\n                    <div class='p-2 rounded bg-slate-800 font-mono text-cyan-300'>\n                        5,000 ÷ 25 = 200 packets<br>\n                        Check: 200 × 25 = 5,000\n                    </div>\n                    <p class='text-emerald-400 font-bold'>Correct Option: 200 packets</p>\n                </div>\n                "}]}]
